eam key (`.p8` and Key ID, plus the Issuer ID in its `Issuer ID` field and in the `APPLE_ISSUER_ID` environment variable, which Apple shows near the top of App Store Connect → Users and Access → Integrations tab, in the format of its documented example `57246542-96fe-1a63-e053-0824d011072a`; for this team it is the developer ID shown in App Store Connect → Users and Access and recorded in PROIOS-9, not the Apple Account (team key authenticated with it, 401 with the Apple Account, 23/11/2026); its role bounds what it can do, across every app of the team), and `APP_STORE_CONNECT_INDIVIDUAL`, the individual key (`.p8`, Key ID; it inherits the associated user's roles and app access and cannot reach provisioning, Sales and Finance, or `notaryTool`). Read `APPLE_ISSUER_ID`, `APPLE_KEY_ID`, and `APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY` from the local environment at call time. The signing code reads the private key in memory to generate the JWT. On Windows, processes can receive user-scoped environment variables, and child processes inherit their parent process's environment by default. For local JWT generation, keep the private key and JWT out of files, command-line arguments, logs, and published artifacts. See [Apple's API token signing instructions](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/generating-tokens-for-api-requests) and [Windows environment variable inheritance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/environment-variables). The `APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY` variable contains the full `.p8` PEM of the team key. Read it from the environment to avoid repeated `op` reads, which prompt the operator on every call (operator decision, 23/11/2026). Keep the key and JWT out of command output, Issues, and Pull Requests. The Sign in with Apple key (a separate key type, bound to the primary App ID `dev.lcv.astrologo`) is kept only in 1Password, never in an environment variable (operator decision, 23/11/2026), and feeds only the Firebase `apple.com` provider's OAuth c