AGENTS.md:13:This text is a body of rules and procedures. It does not restate official documentation: every command, flag, API field, and procedure links to its official page, and that page, or the installed CLI help, is the reference for how the command works.
AGENTS.md:39:* **Rules Match the Official Documentation (operator decision):** every rule, command, flag, and procedure in this text is supported by the official documentation or the installed CLI help of the system where it runs, and links to it. A rule the system does not support is a defect: report it to the operator with the documentation evidence and correct the text if the operator authorizes it; never improvise a workaround.
AGENTS.md:41:* **Zero Guessing and Zero Confabulation:** never infer, presume, or fabricate APIs, CLI flags, parameters, system states, or business rules. Every assertion rests on verifiable live state (`origin/main`, the exact remote SHA, raw API responses) or official documentation. A local working tree is never proof of remote state.
AGENTS.md:43:* **Terminal Pagination and Exhaustive Research:** before declaring any item absent, declaring that no solution exists, or interrupting the operator for missing data, delimit the eligible universe and traverse every relevant surface to its last page: repository records, operator decisions, recorded memories and sessions, chat logs, deep comments, nested links, open and closed Issues, releases and changelogs, current official documentation, technical forums and communities, and the Internet Archive. On a GraphQL `"Query too complex"` error, split the query into a list query plus detail calls ([GraphQL limits](https://docs.github.com/en/graphql/overview/rate-limits-and-query-limits-for-the-graphql-api)). Consult the operator only when the ambiguity survives that traversal.
AGENTS.md:70:  * **Linear:** the Linear GraphQL API ([https://linear.app/developers/graphql](https://linear.app/developers/graphql)) is the only access channel; every agent consults that documentation page to learn the exact schema, queries, and mutations before calling them. Never use a Linear CLI or the Linear MCP server.
AGENTS.md:71:  * **Composio:** Use the [Composio REST API v3.1](https://docs.composio.dev/reference) and follow the authentication requirements documented for each endpoint. For endpoints that accept a user API key, read `COMPOSIO_USER_API_KEY` at call time and send it in `x-user-api-key`. For endpoints that require a project API key, use the configured key for that project in `x-api-key` with the permissions required by the endpoint. See [Composio authentication](https://docs.composio.dev/reference/authenticating-to-composio) and the [connected-accounts listing endpoint](https://docs.composio.dev/reference/api-reference/connected-accounts/getConnectedAccounts). Consult the endpoint documentation for the exact path and payload before calling it. Never use the Composio CLI, a Composio MCP server, or the Composio SDK meta-tools.
AGENTS.md:72:  * **GitHub:** the GitHub GraphQL API ([https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/graphql](https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/graphql)) is the primary access channel; the `gh` CLI is an allowed auxiliary channel. Every agent consults the GraphQL documentation to learn the exact schema before querying. Never use the GitHub MCP server.
AGENTS.md:73:  * **Slack:** the Slack APIs ([https://docs.slack.dev/apis/](https://docs.slack.dev/apis/)) — chiefly the Web API — are the only access channel; every agent consults that documentation page before calling. Never use a Slack MCP server. For bot operations, read `SLACK_AUTH_TOKEN` at call time and send the bot token as `Authorization: Bearer`. For app manifest operations, use the token type documented for the method. For example, `apps.manifest.update` uses an app configuration token with `app_configurations:write`. See [Slack token types](https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/tokens/) and [`apps.manifest.update`](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/apps.manifest.update/). The bot token (`xoxb-`) is issued by the Slack app registered at [https://api.slack.com/apps](https://api.slack.com/apps). Keep tokens out of source code, files, and logs.
AGENTS.md:74:  * **Firebase:** the official Firebase REST APIs ([https://firebase.google.com/docs/reference](https://firebase.google.com/docs/reference)) are the primary access channel; every agent consults that documentation before calling. The Firebase CLI is an allowed auxiliary channel for deploy, emulators, and compiled rules, and the `gcloud` CLI is an allowed auxiliary channel for the underlying Google Cloud services. For administrative Firebase and Google Cloud REST calls, use the `gcloud` access token and send `X-Goog-User-Project: lcv-ideas-and-software`. To validate Firestore Security Rules for an authenticated client, send the test identity's Firebase Authentication ID token. To validate anonymous access, send an unauthenticated request. See [Firestore REST authentication and authorization](https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/use-rest-api#authentication_and_authorization) and [Google Cloud quota project configuration](https://cloud.google.com/docs/quotas/set-quota-project). Never use the Firebase MCP server (`firebase-tools mcp`).
AGENTS.md:75:  * **Cloudflare:** the Cloudflare REST API v4 and the GraphQL Analytics API ([https://developers.cloudflare.com/api/](https://developers.cloudflare.com/api/)) are the primary access channels; every agent consults that documentation before calling. The Wrangler CLI is an allowed auxiliary channel, including the Section 3 exception for regenerating `worker-configuration.d.ts`. Never use the Cloudflare MCP server. The credential is the user API token in the `CLOUDFLARE_USER_TOKEN` environment variable, sent as `Authorization: Bearer`.
AGENTS.md:76:  * **Apple:** the App Store Connect API ([https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi)), served at `https://api.appstoreconnect.apple.com`, is the primary access channel for App Store Connect (apps, builds, TestFlight, in-app purchases, metadata, submissions) of team `P94C797235` (the Team ID; an Enrollment ID is never a Team ID); every agent consults that documentation for the exact resource, path version (`/v1`, `/v2`), filters, and required roles before calling, and follows `links.next` to the last page ([paged document links](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/pageddocumentlinks)). `xcodebuild` with the same API key is an allowed auxiliary channel for signing and uploading builds on the GitHub macOS runners ([upload builds](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-builds/upload-builds/)). Every call carries `Authorization: Bearer <JWT>`, an ES256 JWT signed locally with the key's `.p8` private key: header `kid` = Key ID and `typ: "JWT"`; payload `aud: "appstoreconnect-v1"`, `iat`, `exp` no more than 20 minutes after `iat` except where Apple documents otherwise, `iss` = Issuer ID for a team key or `sub: "user"` without `iss` for an individual key, and an optional `scope` listing the allowed GET requests, kept short because a long list is refused with `403 FORBIDDEN.SCOPE_TOO_LARGE` (observed 23/11/2026), so one token per request ([generating tokens](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/generating-tokens-for-api-requests)). The credentials are two items in the 1Password vault `Employee`, each holding the private key in its `credencial` field and as the attached `.p8`, whose file name carries the Key ID (`AuthKey_<KEY_ID>.p8`, `ApiKey_<KEY_ID>.p8`) ([creating API keys](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/creating-api-keys-for-app-store-connect-api)): `APP_STORE_CONNECT_EQUIPE`, the team key (`.p8` and Key ID, plus the Issuer ID in its `Issuer ID` field and in the `APPLE_ISSUER_ID` environment variable, which Apple shows near the top of App Store Connect → Users and Access → Integrations tab, in the format of its documented example `57246542-96fe-1a63-e053-0824d011072a`; for this team it is the developer ID shown in App Store Connect → Users and Access and recorded in PROIOS-9, not the Apple Account (team key authenticated with it, 401 with the Apple Account, 23/11/2026); its role bounds what it can do, across every app of the team), and `APP_STORE_CONNECT_INDIVIDUAL`, the individual key (`.p8`, Key ID; it inherits the associated user's roles and app access and cannot reach provisioning, Sales and Finance, or `notaryTool`). Read `APPLE_ISSUER_ID`, `APPLE_KEY_ID`, and `APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY` from the local environment at call time. The signing code reads the private key in memory to generate the JWT. On Windows, processes can receive user-scoped environment variables, and child processes inherit their parent process's environment by default. For local JWT generation, keep the private key and JWT out of files, command-line arguments, logs, and published artifacts. See [Apple's API token signing instructions](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/generating-tokens-for-api-requests) and [Windows environment variable inheritance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/environment-variables). The `APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY` variable contains the full `.p8` PEM of the team key. Read it from the environment to avoid repeated `op` reads, which prompt the operator on every call (operator decision, 23/11/2026). Keep the key and JWT out of command output, Issues, and Pull Requests. The Sign in with Apple key (a separate key type, bound to the primary App ID `dev.lcv.astrologo`) is kept only in 1Password, never in an environment variable (operator decision, 23/11/2026), and feeds only the Firebase `apple.com` provider's OAuth code flow, whose Services ID is `dev.lcv.firebase.authentication`. Each iOS repository keeps its own App Store Connect team API key as a repository secret. When an authorized job signs or uploads a build on a GitHub-hosted macOS runner, use that key to authenticate `xcodebuild` and pass a job-scoped temporary `.p8` file to `-authenticationKeyPath`. Xcode signs the app with the applicable signing certificate and provisioning profile. See [Xcode authentication and signing](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode-release-notes/xcode-13-release-notes) and [Apple cloud-managed certificates](https://developer.apple.com/help/account/certificates/cloud-managed-certificates/). Never use a shared controller for these jobs (Architectural Isolation above). Status codes, rate limits, and the first read test are in PROIOS-9 ([errors](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/interpreting-and-handling-errors), [rate limits](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appstoreconnectapi/identifying-rate-limits)).
AGENTS.md:147:  Run `git commit --amend --no-edit -S` and `git push --force-with-lease origin <branch>` ([git push](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push)). Reason recorded in `LCV-517` (observed behavior, not described in GitHub's documentation): Secret Scanning stays pending (`"Waiting for secret scan to complete..."`) when the first commit was pushed before the Pull Request existed; the amend and force push after creation emit `pull_request.synchronize`, which completes Secret Scanning and triggers CodeQL and Code Quality. Entire keeps the checkpoint link across the amend through `post-rewrite`. Verify with `git ls-remote origin "refs/entire/checkpoints/*" | Select-String -SimpleMatch "<CHECKPOINT_ID>"` ([git ls-remote](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-ls-remote)) in the ref-based layout, or `entire checkpoint list --json` in the `entire/checkpoints/v1` layout, and `git log -1 --show-signature`. The head left by the handshake is the first head under review; from then on Section 5.G, item 4, applies.
AGENTS.md:154:  3. **The operator merges, or orders the agent to merge (operator decision);** without that order, hand the operator the pull request with every gate clear and the squash body file below. When ordered: GitHub builds the squash message from `squash_merge_commit_message` (`COMMIT_MESSAGES` on the fleet, `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo> --jq .squash_merge_commit_message`), and any `--body` replaces it entirely, observed behavior not described in GitHub's documentation (`admin-app` #810 reached `main` without its `Entire-Checkpoint:` lines; [squash commit message](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/configuring-pull-request-merges/configuring-commit-squashing-for-pull-requests), [repositories API](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/repos/repos)). By operator decision, write `temp/<pr>-squash-body.md`: the PR body with every trailer line and every workflow-skip instruction removed ([skipping workflow runs](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/manage-workflow-runs/skip-workflow-runs)), a blank line, then the `Entire-Checkpoint:` and `Co-authored-by:` lines read from the PR's own commits (matched case-insensitively, as Git reads trailers, [git interpret-trailers](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-interpret-trailers)); a copied line that carries a workflow-skip instruction stops the merge and is reported. Then `gh pr merge <pr> --squash --body-file temp/<pr>-squash-body.md` ([gh pr merge](https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_pr_merge)) and confirm through the API that the new `main` commit carries those lines. Never `--delete-branch`: the fleet has `delete_branch_on_merge` set to `true` (`gh api repos/<owner>/<repo> --jq .delete_branch_on_merge`; if `false`, `git push origin --delete <agent>/<task>` after the merge).
